{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790969127,"nonce":"wdtoZOCrXWdvqhYeBWtwXgQu1Kk=","title":"Native memory owned by finalizer blocks stays resident a GC cycle longer than on stock HashLink","message":"An HDLL handle that is a MEM_KIND_FINALIZER block owning native memory (a\nmalloc'd payload its finalizer frees) keeps that memory far longer under ash\nthan under stock HashLink. After hl.Gc.major(), a whole round of such handles\nis still resident.\n\nReproducer (no other dependency): ashui's tests/fixtures/ash/finalizer_memory\n(memrepro.c, MemRepro.hx, run.sh). Each handle is\n\n    typedef struct { void (*finalize)(void *); void *payload; } handle;\n    // make(bytes): hl_gc_alloc_finalizer(sizeof(handle)), payload = malloc + memset\n\nand the program makes 50k handles of 16 KB per round, calls hl.Gc.major()\nbetween rounds, and prints RSS after each round (8 rounds):\n\n    ash hybrid (default)   847 861 865 865 865 865 866 868 MB\n    ash interp             808 816 817 818 816 817 818 817 MB\n    ash jit                822 829 826 825 825 824 823 824 MB\n    stock HashLink 1.15     15 120 120 120 120 120 120  82 MB\n    stock HashLink 2.0       4   4   4   4   4   4   4   4 MB\n\nash: local release build at 4d01e44, macOS arm64. Stock: upstream builds on\nthe NUC, Linux x86_64. One round is 50k x 16 KB = 800 MB, so under ash\nroughly a full round's payloads survive the major collection that follows it.\n\nTwo things seem to combine:\n- an unreachable finalizer block is finalized one collection after the one\n  that finds it dead (take_dead_finalizers keeps the block for one more cycle),\n  so the major collection at the end of a round frees the previous round,\n  not this one;\n- collections during a round are too rare to release payloads as allocation\n  proceeds, because the trigger sees only the 16-byte blocks; the native\n  memory behind them is invisible to it (see 8fe55c2 on the trigger).\n\nPossible directions: finalize blocks found dead in the same collection where\nit is safe to, as stock HashLink does; and let native code report memory a\nhandle owns (an ash extension; HashLink has no API for it), so the trigger\ncounts it.\n\nFound through ashui: a Blinc LayoutTree is about 15 KB of native memory behind\na 16-byte handle, and 50k collected trees per round sit near 1 GB under ash\nagainst about 100 MB on stock HashLink. ashui now has LayoutTree.dispose() for\nbulk cases, but any HDLL with large native handles hits this.","files":null}]}